"""Units that close the remaining gaps in coverage of the Catechism's four parts, written in full."""
from spec import L, U

EXTRA = {
'4': [U('The Creed completed', 'Creed', 'What else do we say when we say “I believe”?', [
 L('Almighty Father: God’s power and care', 'God is almighty because nothing is impossible for him, and his power is always used with love. He is Father to all he made and watches over everything he made, which is called providence.', '268,269,270,271,272,273,274,302,303,304,305', 'Luke 1:37;Matthew 6:25-34', 'god creator', 'What does it mean to call God almighty?', 'Make two posters: “Things only God can do” and “How God uses his power to love.” Find one example of each in the Bible.', 'pater',
   hook='Ask: “If you could do anything, what would you do first?” Then say that God can do anything, and ask what he does with that power.',
   teach=['God is almighty (CCC 268–274). “Nothing is impossible with God” (Luke 1:37). His power is shown most in mercy and forgiveness (CCC 270).', 'God is our Father and he cares for creation, which is called providence (CCC 302–305). He guides everything toward its goal without taking away our freedom.', 'Evil and suffering are real, and God does not cause evil. He is Father even when we cannot see it. Jesus trusted the Father even on the cross (CCC 306–314).', 'Teach with warmth. Some children have lost a parent or suffer at home. Say that God is close to all of them.'],
   talk=['What do you think “almighty” means?', 'How does a loving father use his strength?', 'When is it hard to trust that God cares?'],
   misc=['“Almighty means God does whatever he feels like.”', '“If God is almighty, nothing bad could happen.”'],
   quiz=['“Almighty” means that…|Nothing is impossible for God|God is angry|God is far away|God is tired|0|CCC 268.', 'God’s providence means he…|Cares for and guides all he made|Forgot about us|Only watches|Is made of matter|0|CCC 302.', 'God shows his power most in…|Mercy and forgiveness|Punishing|Hiding|Anger|0|CCC 277.'],
   life='Pray the Our Father slowly each night and thank God for one way he took care of you today.', saint='Thérèse of Lisieux: she trusted God like a little child trusts her father.', mem='“With God all things are possible.” (Matthew 19:26)', assess='Ask children to say what almighty means and one way God cares for them.'),
 L('He descended to the dead', 'After Jesus died, his body lay in the tomb and his soul went to the place of the dead to free the holy people who waited for him. Holy Saturday is a day of quiet waiting before Easter.', '632,633,634,635,636,637', '1 Peter 3:18-20;Matthew 27:57-66', 'paschal-mystery redemption', 'What does it mean that Jesus descended to the dead?', 'Mark Holy Saturday in a timeline from Good Friday to Easter. Draw the open doors of the tomb and the people who were waiting. Discuss why Saturday is quiet.', '',
   hook='Light a candle and then cover it. Ask: “Is the light gone?” Wait. Then uncover it and say that the day between the Cross and Easter is like that.',
   teach=['Jesus really died. His body was in the tomb, and his soul went to the “abode of the dead,” called Sheol or Hades (CCC 632–633).', 'He did not go to the hell of the damned but to free the righteous who had died before him, from Adam on (CCC 633–634). An old Easter icon shows him taking Adam and Eve by the hands.', 'Holy Saturday is the Church’s quiet day. There is no Mass, and we wait at the tomb in hope (CCC 635–637).', 'Children may ask about people who died long ago. Explain that Christ’s saving work reaches all of history.'],
   talk=['Why do you think the Church is quiet on Holy Saturday?', 'What does it show that Jesus went to find those who had died?', 'How is waiting in hope different from waiting with fear?'],
   misc=['“Jesus went to hell to suffer.”', '“Jesus’ soul left him for good.”'],
   quiz=['Where did Jesus’ soul go after he died?|To the place of the dead to free the righteous|Straight to heaven|To the place of the damned|Nowhere|0|CCC 633.', 'What day do we keep as a day of quiet waiting?|Holy Saturday|Palm Sunday|Pentecost|Easter Monday|0|CCC 636.', 'An old Easter icon shows Jesus…|Pulling Adam and Eve from the tomb|Sleeping|Writing|Crying|0|Anastasis icon.'],
   life='Take a few quiet minutes on Holy Saturday and pray for those who have died.', saint='John Damascene: an early Christian writer who wrote hymns about Christ’s victory over death.', mem='“He descended into hell; on the third day he rose again from the dead.” (Apostles’ Creed)', assess='Ask children to tell what Jesus did between Friday and Sunday.'),
 L('Amen: our yes to God', '“Amen” means “so be it” and “I believe it.” We say it at the end of the Creed and our prayers to say yes to God. The Creed ends with the hope of life everlasting.', '1061,1062,1063,1064,1065,1020', 'Revelation 3:14;2 Corinthians 1:20', 'creed faith', 'Why do we say Amen?', 'Say a prayer together and put in a loud, joyful Amen. Draw a banner with the word AMEN and a prayer you will say it to.', 'creed',
   hook='Ask a question with a clear answer, such as “Is Jesus risen?” and have the class answer together. Say that this kind of answer is an Amen.',
   teach=['“Amen” is a Hebrew word for “so be it” and “it is true” (CCC 1061–1065). It is the last word of the Bible (Revelation 22:21) and of the Creed.', 'Jesus is called “the Amen,” the faithful witness (Revelation 3:14). All God’s promises find their “Yes” in him (2 Corinthians 1:20).', 'We say Amen at Communion when we say yes to Jesus. It means “I believe it is you.”', 'Teach children to say it clearly and with meaning.'],
   talk=['When do we say Amen at Mass?', 'Why is it good to say it out loud?', 'What would you like to say Amen to?'],
   misc=['“Amen is just a way to end a prayer.”', '“It only means the end.”'],
   quiz=['“Amen” means…|So be it, I believe|Goodbye|Stop|Maybe|0|CCC 1061.', 'At Communion we say Amen to…|Jesus in the Eucharist|The song|The sermon|The end|0|CCC 1396.', 'Who is called “the Amen”?|Jesus|Peter|Moses|Noah|0|Revelation 3:14.'],
   life='Say Amen carefully at every prayer this week, and notice what you are saying yes to.', saint='Justin Martyr: he wrote that the people answer “Amen” at the Eucharist.', mem='“For all the promises of God find their Yes in him. That is why we utter the Amen through him.” (2 Corinthians 1:20)', assess='Ask children to explain what Amen means and use it in a prayer.')])],
'5': [U('Praying the Our Father', 'Prayer', 'What do we ask when we pray the prayer Jesus gave us?', [
 L('Our Father: hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come', 'We begin by calling God Father and by asking that his name be honored and his kingdom come. The first requests are for God’s glory before our own needs.', '2779,2780,2781,2782,2783,2784,2785,2786,2807,2808,2809,2810,2816,2817,2818', 'Matthew 6:9-10;Romans 8:15', 'our-father prayer kingdom-of-god', 'Why do we ask for God’s name and kingdom first?', 'Illustrate “hallowed be thy name” and “thy kingdom come” with a picture of someone honoring God’s name and a picture of a place where God’s kingdom is growing.', 'pater',
   hook='Say the names of three people you love and watch the children’s faces. Ask: “How do you treat the name of someone you love?” Then introduce God’s name.',
   teach=['“Our Father” calls God by the name Jesus used (Abba). We pray as his children, and we pray with and for everyone because it says “our” (CCC 2779–2793).', '“Hallowed be thy name” asks that God’s name be kept holy by us (CCC 2807–2815). We honor his name by how we speak and live.', '“Thy kingdom come” asks for Christ’s reign to grow in hearts and to come in glory at the end (CCC 2816–2821). Lived out: be loving where you are.', 'Help children see these first requests change our hearts.'],
   talk=['Why does Jesus teach us to say “Our” and not “My”?', 'How can we hallow God’s name at school?', 'Where do you see God’s kingdom growing?'],
   misc=['“The Our Father is a list of wishes.”', '“God’s kingdom is only in heaven.”'],
   quiz=['The first three requests in the Our Father are about…|God|Food|Friends|School|0|CCC 2803.', '“Hallowed” means…|Holy|Hollow|Hungry|Hidden|0|CCC 2807.', 'We say “Our” because we pray…|With and for everyone|Alone|Only for family|Only for friends|0|CCC 2792.'],
   life='Say the Our Father each morning slowly, stopping at “thy kingdom come” to name one way you can help it come.', saint='Francis of Assisi: he prayed the Our Father with great love and wrote a paraphrase of it.', mem='Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come.', assess='Ask children to explain the first two requests in their own words.'),
 L('Thy will be done; give us this day our daily bread', 'We ask that God’s will be done on earth as in heaven, and then ask for what we need today. We trust that the Father gives us enough and we share it with others.', '2822,2823,2824,2825,2826,2827,2828,2829,2830,2831,2832,2833,2834,2835,2836,2837', 'Matthew 6:10-11;John 6:35', 'our-father prayer eucharist', 'What do we ask for in daily bread?', 'Make a “daily bread” collage: things we need each day, and one thing we can do to share bread with someone who has none. Add a loaf of bread as a sign of the Eucharist.', 'pater',
   hook='Break a loaf of bread and share it. Ask: “What would happen if no one had any today?”',
   teach=['“Thy will be done” asks to love what God wants, as Jesus did in Gethsemane (Luke 22:42; CCC 2822–2827). God’s will is that all be saved.', '“Give us this day our daily bread” asks for what we need and teaches trust (CCC 2828–2831). The word “our” means we ask for everyone and share with the poor (CCC 2830).', 'The bread is also the Word of God and the Eucharist (CCC 2835–2837). Jesus said, “I am the bread of life.”', 'Avoid making prayer sound like a vending machine. Trust is the heart.'],
   talk=['Why is it hard to say “thy will be done”?', 'What does it mean to ask only for today’s bread?', 'How can you help a family who needs daily bread?'],
   misc=['“If I pray for bread, it falls from the sky.”', '“God’s will is always sad.”'],
   quiz=['“Daily bread” stands for…|What we need and the Eucharist|Only a sandwich|Money|A treat|0|CCC 2835.', 'Who prayed “not my will but yours” in the garden?|Jesus|Peter|Moses|David|0|Luke 22:42.', 'We say “give us” because we pray for…|Everyone|Only me|Only family|No one|0|CCC 2830.'],
   life='Give thanks before each meal this week, and share some food or time with someone in need.', saint='Vincent de Paul: he organized help for the hungry and poor in the name of the Father.', mem='Give us this day our daily bread.', assess='Ask children to say what each request means and one way to share bread.'),
 L('Forgive us; lead us not into temptation; deliver us from evil', 'We ask God to forgive us as we forgive others, to keep us from temptation and to rescue us from evil. The Our Father ends in trust that the Father protects us.', '2838,2839,2840,2841,2842,2843,2844,2845,2846,2847,2848,2849,2850,2851,2852,2853,2854,2855,2856,2857,2858,2859,2860,2861,2862,2863,2864,2865', 'Matthew 6:12-15;Matthew 6:13', 'our-father prayer forgiveness mercy', 'Why must we forgive in order to be forgiven?', 'Role-play a time someone says sorry and the other forgives. Draw a shield for “deliver us from evil” and write the names of those who help protect us.', 'pater',
   hook='Tell a short story about two children who fight over a toy. Ask: “What would make it right?”',
   teach=['“Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us” asks mercy for ourselves and gives us the duty to forgive (CCC 2838–2845). Jesus says, “If you do not forgive others, neither will your Father forgive you.”', '“Lead us not into temptation” asks that God not let us be led into sin and that he give strength (CCC 2846–2849). God never tempts anyone (James 1:13). He helps us choose well.', '“Deliver us from evil” asks God to rescue us from the Evil One (CCC 2850–2854). The last words, “For the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours,” are a doxology added at Mass (CCC 2855).', 'Be gentle with children who are hurt. Forgiving does not mean staying with someone who is unsafe.'],
   talk=['Why is it hard to forgive?', 'What helps you when you are tempted?', 'Who helps protect you from evil?'],
   misc=['“God leads people into sin to test them.”', '“Forgiving means saying that nothing happened.”'],
   quiz=['Does God tempt us to sin?|No, he helps us resist|Yes, to test us|Sometimes|Only on Sunday|0|James 1:13.', 'We ask forgiveness “as we forgive” because…|Our mercy shows our hearts are open|It is a rule with no reason|God needs help|It is easy|0|CCC 2843.', 'The last request is for…|Deliverance from evil|Good grades|A prize|A trip|0|CCC 2850.'],
   life='Forgive someone this week, or ask forgiveness, and pray the whole Our Father at bedtime.', saint='Maria Goretti: she forgave the man who attacked her before she died.', mem='Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us; and lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.', assess='Ask children to name the last three requests and say what each asks.')])],
'6': [U('The first three commandments', 'Morality', 'How do we love God above all?', [
 L('You shall have no other gods', 'The first commandment calls us to love God above everything and to put nothing in his place. We worship God alone and avoid idols, superstition and fear of luck.', '2083,2084,2085,2086,2087,2088,2089,2090,2096,2097,2110,2111,2112,2113,2114', 'Exodus 20:2-6;Deuteronomy 6:4-5', 'commandments faith hope charity adoration', 'What does it mean to put God first?', 'List things people put first (sports, popularity, money, devices). Draw a ladder with God at the top and talk about how to keep the order.', 'before-study',
   hook='Ask students to rank four things from most to least important in their lives. Ask what happens when the order changes.',
   teach=['The first commandment: “I am the Lord your God… you shall have no other gods before me” (CCC 2083–2094). It calls for faith, hope and love of God above all.', 'We adore God alone and worship him (CCC 2096–2100). Sins against it: idolatry, superstition, divination, magic, tempting God, irreligion and atheism (CCC 2110–2128).', 'Idols today can be money, fame, pleasure or technology (CCC 2113). Even good things become idols when they come first.', 'Images of Christ and the saints are not idols; we honor the person, not the object (CCC 2131–2132).'],
   talk=['What things try to take first place in your life?', 'What is the difference between honoring a saint and worshiping God?', 'Why is horoscope-reading a problem for a Christian?'],
   misc=['“Horoscopes are just a game.”', '“Statues are idols.”'],
   quiz=['The first commandment asks us to…|Love God above all|Take a rest|Honor parents|Tell the truth|0|CCC 2083.', 'An idol is…|Anything we put before God|A statue only|A saint|A prayer|0|CCC 2113.', 'Praying to a saint is…|Asking for help, not worship|Worship|Wrong|Magic|0|CCC 956.'],
   life='Choose one thing that takes too much of your time and give some of that time to prayer or service.', saint='Thomas More: he put God first even at the cost of his life.', mem='“You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart.” (Deuteronomy 6:5)', assess='Ask students to explain the first commandment and give a modern example of an idol.'),
 L('You shall not take the name of the Lord in vain', 'The second commandment asks us to respect God’s name and the names of holy people and things. We use God’s name to pray, bless and praise, not to curse or swear falsely.', '2142,2143,2144,2145,2146,2147,2148,2149,2150,2151,2152,2153,2154,2155,2156,2157,2158,2159,2160,2161,2162,2163,2164,2165,2166,2167', 'Exodus 20:7;Philippians 2:9-11', 'commandments prayer', 'How can we honor God’s name?', 'Write a list of ways to use God’s name well (prayer, blessing, thanks) and ways to avoid misuse. Practice the Sign of the Cross with reverence and say the name of Jesus slowly.', 'jesus-prayer',
   hook='Ask: “How would you feel if a stranger shouted your name as an insult?” Introduce the second commandment.',
   teach=['“You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain” (CCC 2142–2149). God’s name is holy, and we show respect in speech and silence.', 'Sins against it: blasphemy, cursing, careless use of God’s name, false oaths (perjury) (CCC 2148–2155). Oaths and promises made in God’s name must be kept (CCC 2150–2155).', 'The name of Jesus is powerful in prayer: “At the name of Jesus every knee should bow” (Philippians 2:10; CCC 2666–2668). We bless God’s name in the Sign of the Cross and our personal name given in Baptism is holy (CCC 2156–2159).', 'Treat this topic with understanding: many children have heard careless language at home. Teach what to say instead.'],
   talk=['Why do you think people use God’s name carelessly?', 'What can you say instead?', 'How does the Sign of the Cross honor God’s name?'],
   misc=['“OMG is harmless.”', '“Only swear words break the commandment.”'],
   quiz=['The second commandment forbids…|Misusing God’s name|Missing Sunday|Stealing|Lying|0|CCC 2142.', 'A false oath is called…|Perjury|Prayer|Praise|Fasting|0|CCC 2152.', 'We honor God’s name when we…|Pray and bless|Curse|Shout|Hide|0|CCC 2143.'],
   life='Catch yourself using God’s name carelessly and replace it with a short prayer.', saint='Bernardine of Siena: he spread devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus.', mem='“At the name of Jesus every knee should bow.” (Philippians 2:10)', assess='Ask students to explain the second commandment and two ways to honor God’s name.'),
 L('Remember the Sabbath: keep the Lord’s Day holy', 'The third commandment asks us to set aside a day for God. Christians keep Sunday, the day of the Resurrection, with Mass, rest, family and works of mercy.', '2168,2169,2170,2171,2172,2173,2174,2175,2176,2177,2178,2179,2180,2181,2182,2183,2184,2185,2186,2187,2188,2189,2190,2191,2192,2193,2194,2195', 'Exodus 20:8-11;Mark 2:27-28', 'commandments sabbath sunday mass', 'Why do Christians keep Sunday?', 'Plan a Lord’s Day that includes Mass, rest, a family activity and one work of mercy. Compare with how your family spends Sundays now.', '',
   hook='Ask: “When was the last time you had a whole day to rest with no rush?” Let them describe it.',
   teach=['The Sabbath recalls creation and the Exodus (CCC 2168–2172). Jesus said, “The Sabbath was made for man” (Mark 2:27), and he healed on it.', 'Christians keep Sunday because Jesus rose on the first day of the week (CCC 2174–2176). Sunday Mass is required for Catholics unless a serious reason prevents it (CCC 2180–2183).', 'We rest from unnecessary work to worship, enjoy family, and perform works of mercy (CCC 2184–2188). Rest respects human dignity.', 'Be sensitive: some families must work on Sundays or lack transportation. Talk about desire for Mass and what they can do.'],
   talk=['What makes a day feel set apart?', 'What activities fill up Sunday that could be left out?', 'What is one work of mercy you could do on Sunday?'],
   misc=['“Sunday is just a day off.”', '“Resting means doing nothing.”'],
   quiz=['Christians keep Sunday because…|Jesus rose on that day|It is the weekend|Moses said so|A king decided|0|CCC 2174.', 'The Church requires Catholics to…|Attend Mass on Sundays and holy days|Fast every Sunday|Never work|Pray all day|0|CCC 2180.', '“The Sabbath was made for man” means…|Rest is a gift|Rest is a punishment|Work is bad|It does not matter|0|Mark 2:27.'],
   life='Choose one thing to leave undone on Sunday so there is room for rest, family and Mass.', saint='Dominic Savio: a boy who loved Mass and set aside time for God.', mem='“Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy.” (Exodus 20:8)', assess='Ask students to explain why Sunday matters and plan one practice for it.')])],
'7': [U('Honoring family and respecting life', 'Morality', 'How does love shape our family and our respect for each person?', [
 L('Honor your father and mother', 'The fourth commandment asks children to honor and obey parents, and asks parents and leaders to serve those in their care. The family is the first school of love, and honor continues as parents age.', '2196,2197,2198,2199,2200,2201,2202,2203,2204,2205,2206,2214,2215,2216,2217,2218,2219,2220,2221,2222,2223,2224,2225,2226,2227,2228,2229,2230,2231', 'Exodus 20:12;Ephesians 6:1-4', 'commandments obedience', 'What does it mean to honor parents?', 'Write a letter of thanks to a parent, grandparent or guardian. List ways to honor adults who care for you at home, in school and in the parish.', 'st-joseph',
   hook='Ask students to name one thing a family member did for them this week. Say that honoring is first noticing.',
   teach=['The fourth commandment: “Honor your father and mother” (CCC 2196–2200). It is the first with a promise (Ephesians 6:2). Honor includes respect, gratitude, obedience while young and care when they are old (CCC 2214–2220).', 'Parents have duties: to love, teach, protect and respect their children’s freedom (CCC 2221–2231). The family is a “domestic church” (CCC 2204–2206).', 'Obedience has limits: we obey God before any human command, and abuse is never right (CCC 2242). If someone is being harmed, they should tell a trusted adult.', 'Public authorities also have duties to serve the common good (CCC 2234–2246).'],
   talk=['What does honoring a parent look like when you disagree?', 'How can you help your family this week?', 'Who should you tell if someone is being hurt?'],
   misc=['“Honor means always agreeing.”', '“Obedience means I have no voice.”'],
   quiz=['The fourth commandment says to…|Honor your father and mother|Rest on Sunday|Not steal|Not lie|0|Exodus 20:12.', 'The family is called the…|Domestic church|School|Business|Town|0|CCC 2204.', 'We must obey God…|Before any human command|After everyone else|Never|Only at church|0|CCC 2242.'],
   life='Do one unrequested job at home and thank a parent or guardian in words.', saint='Joseph: he guarded and provided for Jesus and Mary.', mem='“Honor your father and your mother, that your days may be long in the land.” (Exodus 20:12)', assess='Ask students to explain honor and two ways they will show it.'),
 L('You shall not kill: the gift of every life', 'Every life is a gift from God, from conception to natural death. The fifth commandment forbids murder and calls us to protect life, make peace, avoid revenge and care for our health and safety.', '2258,2259,2260,2261,2262,2263,2264,2265,2266,2268,2269,2284,2285,2286,2287,2288,2289,2290,2302,2303,2304,2305,2306', 'Exodus 20:13;Matthew 5:21-24', 'commandments sanctity-of-life peace', 'How do we respect every life?', 'Make a “life poster” showing ways to protect and cherish life at every age: care for babies, the sick, the elderly, creation. Add one act of peacemaking.', 'peace',
   hook='Ask: “How can words or actions hurt someone without touching them?” List answers and connect to anger.',
   teach=['Human life is sacred because each person is made in God’s image (CCC 2258–2262). The commandment forbids killing the innocent and teaches us to settle anger peacefully (CCC 2302–2306; Matthew 5:21–22).', 'It covers bullying, hatred, scandal, drugs, reckless risk and neglect of our health (CCC 2284–2291). Taking care of our body is a duty.', 'Jesus calls us to be peacemakers (CCC 2304–2305). Revenge is forbidden; forgiveness is the way.', 'Treat gently: students may have experienced loss or violence. Provide resources.'],
   talk=['What does it mean that all people are made in God’s image?', 'How do you handle anger?', 'Who is easy to overlook when it comes to respect?'],
   misc=['“The fifth commandment is only about murder.”', '“Anger does not matter.”'],
   quiz=['Life is sacred because…|Made in God’s image|We say so|It is useful|It is long|0|CCC 2258.', 'Jesus teaches that anger…|Can lead to sin and needs peace|Is always fine|Is never felt|Is a gift|0|Matthew 5:22.', 'A peacemaker is…|Blessed|Weak|Silly|Alone|0|Matthew 5:9.'],
   life='Make peace with someone you have argued with, and pray for someone who is sick.', saint='Maximilian Kolbe: he gave his life to save a stranger in a concentration camp.', mem='“Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God.” (Matthew 5:9)', assess='Ask students to explain the fifth commandment and name three ways to protect life.'),
 L('Faithful love: purity and the dignity of the body', 'The sixth commandment asks us to respect our bodies and the bodies of others. Chastity is the virtue that helps us love well. God made the body good, and the gift of sexuality belongs to marriage.', '2331,2332,2333,2334,2335,2336,2337,2338,2339,2340,2341,2342,2343,2344,2345,2346,2347,2348,2349,2350', 'Genesis 1:27;1 Corinthians 6:19-20', 'commandments chastity image-of-god', 'What does it mean that my body is a gift?', 'Use age-appropriate materials from your parish or school for this lesson, with parents informed. Work with students to write a “respect pledge” for friendships, language and media.', 'before-study',
   hook='Ask: “What does it mean to treat a gift with care?” Hold up a wrapped gift and connect to the body.',
   teach=['God made us male and female, equal in dignity, and the body is good (Genesis 1:27; CCC 2331–2336). We are a unity of body and soul.', 'Chastity is the integration of sexuality into the whole person (CCC 2337–2347). Everyone is called to chastity according to their state of life (CCC 2348–2350).', 'Modesty protects the intimacy of the person (CCC 2521–2524). We respect others’ boundaries and say no to anything that treats people as objects.', 'Follow your diocese’s guidelines and inform parents. Keep language age-appropriate and dignified. Never ask students for personal disclosures.'],
   talk=['What does respect look like with friends and online?', 'Who are trusted adults you can talk to?', 'Why is it good to have boundaries?'],
   misc=['“The Church thinks the body is bad.”', '“Chastity is only about saying no.”'],
   quiz=['The body is…|A good gift from God|Bad|Unimportant|Only a machine|0|CCC 2332.', 'Chastity is…|Integration of sexuality in the person|Hiding|Fear|Silence|0|CCC 2337.', 'Everyone is called to…|Chastity in their state of life|Silence|Marriage|Nothing|0|CCC 2348.'],
   life='Choose one media habit or friendship boundary to protect your dignity and others’.', saint='Maria Goretti: she defended her purity with courage and forgave her attacker.', mem='“Do you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit?” (1 Corinthians 6:19)', assess='Ask students to define chastity and name one practical boundary.')])],
'8': [U('Justice, truth and contentment', 'Morality', 'How do we love our neighbor in what we have and what we say?', [
 L('You shall not steal: justice and respect for goods', 'The seventh commandment forbids stealing and cheating and asks us to be fair, honest and generous. God gave the earth to everyone, so we share what we have and care for the poor.', '2401,2402,2403,2404,2405,2406,2407,2408,2409,2410,2411,2412,2413,2414,2415,2416,2417,2418,2443,2444,2445,2446', 'Exodus 20:15;Luke 3:10-14', 'commandments justice stewardship', 'What do justice and generosity ask of us?', 'Role-play cases: found money, borrowed items, pirated media, unfair work. Decide what justice requires and how to repair harm.', 'pater',
   hook='Ask: “Is it stealing if no one notices?” Discuss the case of copying homework or downloading a movie illegally.',
   teach=['“You shall not steal” protects the goods of others (CCC 2401–2414). Theft, fraud, cheating, unjust wages and pirating are sins. Restitution repairs the wrong (CCC 2412).', 'The earth’s goods are meant for everyone, so private property has a social purpose (CCC 2402–2406). Giving to the poor is an act of justice (CCC 2446).', 'We care for creation and animals (CCC 2415–2418). John the Baptist told soldiers and tax collectors to be fair (Luke 3:10–14).', 'Be mindful that students may face poverty. Never shame.'],
   talk=['When is it hard to be honest about money or things?', 'How do you repair a wrong?', 'What can we do for people with less?'],
   misc=['“If it’s not hurting anyone it’s not stealing.”', '“Giving to the poor is optional.”'],
   quiz=['Restitution means…|Repairing what was taken|Hiding it|Forgetting|Blaming|0|CCC 2412.', 'The goods of the earth are meant for…|Everyone|A few|Nobody|Kings|0|CCC 2402.', 'Cheating on a test is…|A form of dishonesty|Fine|Smart|Harmless|0|CCC 2409.'],
   life='Return or repay something you owe, and give to someone in need.', saint='Zacchaeus: the tax collector who gave back four times what he had cheated.', mem='“Let him who steals steal no longer, but rather let him labor.” (Ephesians 4:28)', assess='Ask students to explain justice in goods and propose a repair for a case.'),
 L('You shall not bear false witness: truth and trust', 'The eighth commandment asks us to speak the truth in love. Lies, gossip, rash judgment and betraying secrets harm trust. Jesus is the Truth.', '2464,2465,2466,2467,2468,2469,2470,2471,2472,2473,2474,2475,2476,2477,2478,2479,2480,2481,2482,2483,2484,2485,2486,2487,2488,2489,2490,2491,2492,2493,2494,2495,2496,2497,2498,2499,2500,2501,2502,2503,2504,2505,2506,2507,2508,2509,2510,2511,2512,2513', 'Exodus 20:16;John 14:6', 'commandments justice', 'Why does truth matter so much?', 'Sort statements into truth, gossip, lies, calumny, detraction, rash judgment. Write a short, kind way to say a hard truth.', '',
   hook='Play “telephone” with a short sentence and compare the end. Ask what happens to trust when words change.',
   teach=['Jesus is the Truth (John 14:6). We bear witness to him by truthful speech and sometimes at great cost, as martyrs do (CCC 2464–2474).', 'Sins against truth: lying, perjury, rash judgment, detraction, calumny, flattery (CCC 2475–2487). Reparation includes correcting the record.', 'Secrets and privacy are to be respected; the seal of Confession is absolute (CCC 2488–2492). Media and social networks call for honesty and charity (CCC 2493–2499).', 'Truth must be spoken with love: “speaking the truth in love” (Ephesians 4:15).'],
   talk=['When is it hard to tell the truth?', 'What is the difference between gossip and concern?', 'How do we handle truth online?'],
   misc=['“White lies don’t matter.”', '“Truth should be used as a weapon.”'],
   quiz=['Detraction is…|Revealing someone’s faults without good reason|Telling a joke|Praising|Singing|0|CCC 2477.', 'Calumny is…|Lying to harm a reputation|A prayer|A feast|A saint|0|CCC 2477.', 'We should speak the truth…|In love|Cruelly|Never|Quickly|0|Ephesians 4:15.'],
   life='Choose to speak only what is true and kind for a day, and make amends where you gossiped.', saint='Thomas More: he refused to lie under oath and died for the truth.', mem='“Let your yes be yes and your no be no.” (Matthew 5:37)', assess='Ask students to define lying, detraction and calumny and give a repair for each.'),
 L('You shall not covet: freedom of heart', 'The ninth and tenth commandments reach into the heart: purity of heart and freedom from envy and greed. Gratitude, generosity and trust in God make us free.', '2514,2515,2516,2517,2518,2519,2520,2521,2522,2523,2524,2525,2526,2527,2528,2529,2530,2531,2532,2533,2534,2535,2536,2537,2538,2539,2540,2541,2542,2543,2544,2545,2546,2547,2548,2549,2550,2551,2552,2553,2554,2555,2556,2557', 'Exodus 20:17;Matthew 5:8', 'commandments humility gratitude', 'How do we keep a heart free of envy?', 'Write a gratitude list of ten things you have. Then draw a “heart garden” with plants that stand for gratitude, generosity and trust.', 'serenity',
   hook='Show two bags of candy, one with more. Ask students how they feel when they see the difference. Name envy.',
   teach=['The ninth commandment protects purity of heart (CCC 2514–2533). Jesus says, “Blessed are the pure in heart” (Matthew 5:8). Purity needs prayer, modesty and discipline of the senses.', 'The tenth guards against greed and envy (CCC 2534–2557). Envy is sadness at another’s good; it is a capital sin (CCC 2538–2540).', 'The remedy is gratitude, humility and trust in providence. “Where your treasure is, there will be your heart also” (Matthew 6:21; CCC 2544–2547).', 'Teach with compassion. Social media can inflame comparison; invite students to notice it.'],
   talk=['What makes you envious?', 'How can gratitude change how we see things?', 'What does a pure heart look like?'],
   misc=['“Wanting things is always wrong.”', '“Envy is harmless.”'],
   quiz=['Envy is…|Sadness at another’s good|Joy|Prayer|Courage|0|CCC 2539.', 'The antidote to envy is…|Gratitude and generosity|More stuff|Anger|Hiding|0|CCC 2540.', 'Blessed are the pure in heart, for they…|See God|Win|Are rich|Sleep|0|Matthew 5:8.'],
   life='Every night this week write three things you are thankful for and thank a person for a gift.', saint='Francis of Assisi: he gave up wealth and found freedom.', mem='“Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.” (Matthew 5:8)', assess='Ask students to explain the ninth and tenth commandments and a practice that helps.')])],
'9': [U('How the faith grows and is handed on', 'Revelation', 'How does the Church hand on the faith faithfully?', [
 L('Development of doctrine', 'The Church understands the faith more deeply over time while teaching the same truth. Doctrine develops like a seed growing into a tree, never contradicting what came before.', '66,67,94,98,99,100,101,102,103,104,105,106,107', 'John 16:12-13;Matthew 13:31-32', 'doctrine revelation tradition', 'How can teaching grow without changing?', 'Trace the development of one doctrine, like the Trinity or Mary as Mother of God, across councils. Make a timeline showing what was clarified.', 'come-holy-spirit',
   hook='Show a seed and a tree. Ask: “Is the tree something new, or the seed grown up?”',
   teach=['The deposit of faith was complete with the apostles (CCC 66, 84). Over time the Church understands it more fully with the help of the Spirit (CCC 94; Dei Verbum 8).', 'Growth comes through contemplation, study, lived experience and the Magisterium (CCC 94). The Council of Nicaea clarified, not invented, belief in Christ’s divinity.', 'Newman wrote that a true development preserves the same type and principles; a corruption does not (Essay on Development). Use his seven notes as a guide.', 'Distinguish doctrine (which develops) from discipline (which can change). Examples: fasting rules, vernacular Mass.'],
   talk=['Can you think of a teaching that was clarified over time?', 'What is the difference between a development and a change?', 'Why does the Church need a living teacher?'],
   misc=['“The Church changes its teaching whenever it wants.”', '“Nothing in the faith has ever been explained more deeply.”'],
   quiz=['A true development of doctrine…|Keeps the same truth and deepens it|Reverses an earlier teaching|Invents a new revelation|Ignores Scripture|0|CCC 94.', 'Who wrote an influential Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine?|John Henry Newman|Thomas Aquinas|Francis de Sales|Teresa of Ávila|0|Newman, 1845.', 'Which can change?|Discipline|Dogma of the Trinity|The resurrection|The Creed|0|CCC 83.'],
   life='Pick a doctrine and read how it was explained in a council or a Father.', saint='John Henry Newman: a convert and scholar who studied how doctrine grows.', mem='“When the Spirit of truth comes, he will guide you into all the truth.” (John 16:13)', assess='Ask students to explain development of doctrine and distinguish it from change.'),
 L('Dogma and the hierarchy of truths', 'Dogmas are truths the Church teaches as revealed by God. Not all truths are equally central: the heart of the faith is the Trinity, Christ and our salvation. All truths belong together.', '88,89,90,91,92,93,234,426,1066', 'Ephesians 4:11-16;John 8:31-32', 'dogma doctrine heresy', 'What is a dogma and why do we have them?', 'Sort statements into dogma, doctrine, discipline and opinion. Draw a pyramid with the center of the faith at the top and ordered truths below it.', 'creed',
   hook='Ask: “What are the rules that cannot change in a game, and which can?” Link to dogma and discipline.',
   teach=['A dogma is a truth contained in Revelation that the Church defines (CCC 88–90). Dogmas are like lights on the path: they illuminate and secure the faith (CCC 89).', 'There is an order, or “hierarchy,” of truths (CCC 90; Unitatis Redintegratio 11). The mystery of the Trinity and Christ stand at the center (CCC 234, 426).', 'Teachings can be defined (dogma), taught definitively, taught authoritatively, or be pastoral guidance. Respond with the appropriate assent (CCC 891–892).', 'Heresy is the obstinate denial of a revealed truth; it harms unity and faith (CCC 2089).'],
   talk=['Why might a Church define a dogma?', 'What is at the center of our faith?', 'How can a hierarchy of truths help us in dialogue?'],
   misc=['“Dogmas are arbitrary rules.”', '“Some Catholic teachings do not matter.”'],
   quiz=['A dogma is…|A truth the Church defines as revealed|A rule of dress|A song|A feast|0|CCC 88.', 'At the center of the hierarchy of truths is…|The Trinity and Christ|A custom|A building|A holiday|0|CCC 234.', 'Heresy is…|Obstinate denial of a revealed truth|A question|A prayer|A doubt|0|CCC 2089.'],
   life='Choose one dogma and write why it matters for your life.', saint='Athanasius: he held firmly to the dogma of Christ’s divinity at great personal cost.', mem='“You will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.” (John 8:32)', assess='Ask students to explain dogma, hierarchy of truths and heresy.'),
 L('The sense of the faithful and the work of the laity in handing on the faith', 'All the baptized receive the Spirit and share in handing on the faith. The sense of the faithful, parents, teachers and catechists, and the witness of ordinary people help keep the faith alive.', '91,92,93,904,905,906,907,908,909,910,911,912,913,2221,2222,2223,2224,2225', 'John 14:26;1 John 2:20', 'laity holy-spirit evangelization', 'How do ordinary Catholics hand on the faith?', 'Interview a grandparent, catechist or parent about who passed on the faith to them. Write a short thank-you and a plan for passing it on.', '',
   hook='Ask: “Who taught you to pray?” Collect names and notice how many are ordinary people.',
   teach=['The whole body of the faithful, who have an anointing from the Holy One, cannot err in believing (CCC 91–93; 1 John 2:20). This “sense of the faith” works under the guidance of the Magisterium.', 'The laity share in Christ’s prophetic office by witness and teaching (CCC 904–907). Parents are the first teachers (CCC 2221–2226).', 'Catechists, teachers, godparents and friends hand on the faith. Women and men have done this since the first generations.', 'It is not majority opinion. The sense of the faith is discerned by the Church and is linked to Tradition and Scripture.'],
   talk=['Who handed the faith to you?', 'How can you hand on your faith to someone younger?', 'Why is the sense of the faith not the same as a poll?'],
   misc=['“What most Catholics think defines doctrine.”', '“Only priests teach the faith.”'],
   quiz=['Parents are…|The first teachers of the faith|Not involved|Only helpers|Replaced by the school|0|CCC 2223.', 'The sense of the faith means…|The body of the faithful believes the truth under the Spirit|A poll|A feeling|A vote|0|CCC 92.', 'Which is a way to hand on the faith?|Witness and teaching|Silence only|Hiding|Money|0|CCC 905.'],
   life='Write a note of thanks to someone who passed the faith on to you, and share a prayer with someone younger.', saint='Monica: a mother whose prayers helped her son Augustine return to faith.', mem='“But you have been anointed by the Holy One, and you all have knowledge.” (1 John 2:20)', assess='Ask students to explain the sense of the faith and how they will pass on the faith.')])],
'11': [U('The Church’s rites, indulgences and funerals', 'Sacraments', 'How does the Church celebrate and accompany us?', [
 L('Rites, sacramentals and popular devotion', 'The Church celebrates in different rites, such as the Roman, Byzantine and Armenian, and uses sacramentals such as blessings, holy water and medals to sanctify life. Popular devotion expresses the faith of the people.', '1200,1201,1202,1203,1204,1205,1206,1667,1668,1669,1670,1671,1672,1673,1674,1675,1676,1677,1678,1679', 'Psalm 141:2;Colossians 3:16-17', 'liturgy sacramental holy-water', 'What is the difference between a sacrament and a sacramental?', 'Compare two rites using videos or visits. Make a chart of sacraments and sacramentals, and design a family blessing for a home or meal.', 'sign',
   hook='Show holy water, a medal, a rosary and ashes. Ask which are sacraments and which are not.',
   teach=['The mystery of Christ is celebrated in many liturgical traditions: Roman, Byzantine, Alexandrian, Antiochene, Armenian, Chaldean and others. Unity in faith, diversity in rites (CCC 1200–1206).', 'Sacramentals are “sacred signs” instituted by the Church that prepare us for grace (CCC 1667–1673). Examples: blessings, holy water, ashes, candles, medals.', 'Popular piety includes pilgrimages, processions, the Way of the Cross and the Rosary (CCC 1674–1676). It must be guided by the liturgy and not replace it (CCC 1675).', 'Sacramentals are not magic. Their effect depends on faith and the Church’s prayer (CCC 1670).'],
   talk=['What sacramentals are in your home?', 'What does diversity of rites teach about the Church?', 'How do devotions lead us to the sacraments?'],
   misc=['“Medals work like charms.”', '“Only the Roman rite is Catholic.”'],
   quiz=['A sacramental is…|A sacred sign instituted by the Church|A sacrament|A superstition|A prayer book|0|CCC 1667.', 'Examples of sacramentals include…|Holy water and blessings|Baptism|Eucharist|Marriage|0|CCC 1668.', 'Popular piety should…|Be guided by the liturgy|Replace Mass|Be ignored|Be hidden|0|CCC 1675.'],
   life='Bless your home or a meal this week, and learn the meaning of a sacramental you own.', saint='Louis de Montfort: a priest who spread devotions to Mary and the Rosary.', mem='“Let my prayer be counted as incense before you.” (Psalm 141:2)', assess='Ask students to distinguish sacrament, sacramental and devotion with examples.'),
 L('Indulgences, purgatory and the communion of saints', 'An indulgence is the Church’s way of applying Christ’s mercy to remove the temporal punishment due to sin already forgiven. It rests on the communion of saints and requires conversion, Confession, Communion and prayer.', '1030,1031,1032,1471,1472,1473,1474,1475,1476,1477,1478,1479,1498,962,1055', 'Matthew 18:18;2 Maccabees 12:44-46', 'indulgence purgatory communion-of-saints', 'What is an indulgence and what is it not?', 'Study a table of indulgences (partial and plenary). Write a short answer to “Can you buy forgiveness?” using the Catechism, then plan how to gain a plenary indulgence.', 'eternal-rest',
   hook='Break a vase and glue it. Say that forgiveness mends the relationship, but cracks remain and need healing. Introduce temporal punishment.',
   teach=['Sin has two consequences: eternal (removed by forgiveness) and temporal (the need for purification) (CCC 1472–1473). Indulgences remit temporal punishment (CCC 1471).', 'Rooted in the communion of saints: the Church draws on the “treasury” of Christ’s and the saints’ merits (CCC 1475–1477). Conditions: the sacrament of Penance, Communion, prayer for the pope’s intentions, detachment from sin (CCC 1471–1479).', 'Indulgences cannot be bought; the sale of indulgences was an abuse condemned at Trent and abolished by Pius V (1567). Indulgences can be gained for oneself or for the dead (CCC 1498).', 'Link to purgatory (CCC 1030–1032) and to prayer for the dead (2 Maccabees 12:44–46).'],
   talk=['Why is forgiveness not the same as healing?', 'How can we help the dead?', 'Why was the sale of indulgences such a scandal?'],
   misc=['“Indulgences buy forgiveness.”', '“Indulgences forgive sin.”'],
   quiz=['An indulgence remits…|Temporal punishment for forgiven sin|The guilt of sin|Original sin|Nothing|0|CCC 1471.', 'A plenary indulgence requires…|Confession, Communion, prayer for the pope’s intentions|Money|A trip|Nothing|0|CCC 1471.', 'Selling indulgences was…|An abuse|A command|A sacrament|A custom|0|Trent.'],
   life='Offer a prayer or Mass for someone who has died, and gain a plenary indulgence on All Souls with the usual conditions.', saint='John Fisher: a bishop who defended the Church and was martyred.', mem='“Whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven.” (Matthew 18:18)', assess='Ask students to explain indulgences, the conditions and the abuse that was condemned.'),
 L('Christian death and the funeral rites', 'The Church accompanies the dying and the dead with prayer, the sacraments and the funeral Mass. The funeral rites entrust the person to God’s mercy and comfort the grieving with the hope of resurrection.', '1010,1011,1012,1013,1014,1680,1681,1682,1683,1684,1685,1686,1687,1688,1689,1690,1691,1692,1693', 'John 11:25-26;1 Thessalonians 4:13-18', 'eternal-life judgment communion-of-saints', 'How does the Church help us at death?', 'Study the Order of Christian Funerals: vigil, Mass and committal. Write the readings and prayers you would choose, and a letter of condolence.', 'eternal-rest',
   hook='Pause for a moment of silence for someone who has died. Ask: “What helped you most when someone you loved died?”',
   teach=['Death is the end of earthly life, and for those who die in Christ a passing into eternal life (CCC 1010–1014). “For me to live is Christ, and to die is gain” (Philippians 1:21).', 'The funeral has three stations: vigil (wake), funeral Mass, and committal (CCC 1680–1690). The Mass offers the Eucharist for the deceased and entrusts them to God’s mercy.', 'The Church prefers burial and permits cremation if it is not chosen to deny the resurrection; ashes should be buried or entombed, not scattered (CCC 2300; Ad resurgendum cum Christo, 2016).', 'The rites also comfort those who mourn (CCC 1687–1690). Pray for the dead and visit the bereaved.'],
   talk=['What do the white vestments at funerals proclaim?', 'How can we comfort people who grieve?', 'Why does the Church bury or entomb ashes?'],
   misc=['“Funerals are only a celebration of the person’s life.”', '“Scattering ashes is fine.”'],
   quiz=['The funeral Mass offers…|The Eucharist for the deceased|A speech only|A party|A sermon about money|0|CCC 1689.', 'The Church recommends that cremated remains be…|Buried or entombed|Scattered|Kept at home|Divided|0|Ad resurgendum cum Christo.', 'The Christian hope at death is…|Resurrection|Reincarnation|Nothing|Fear|0|CCC 1012.'],
   life='Visit a grave, pray for someone who died and write a note to someone grieving.', saint='Monica: she asked only to be remembered at the altar of the Lord.', mem='“I am the resurrection and the life; he who believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live.” (John 11:25)', assess='Ask students to explain the funeral rites and the Church’s teaching on cremation.')])],
'12': [U('The moral life: acts, law and grace', 'Morality', 'How do law and grace work together in a moral life?', [
 L('The morality of human acts', 'An act is good only if its object, intention and circumstances are good. The end does not justify the means. Passions and feelings are part of being human and are meant to be guided by reason.', '1749,1750,1751,1752,1753,1754,1755,1756,1757,1758,1759,1760,1761,1762,1763,1764,1765,1766,1767,1768,1769,1770,1771,1772,1773,1774,1775', 'Matthew 15:10-20;Romans 3:8', 'conscience free-will virtue', 'What makes an act good or bad?', 'Analyze cases using the three sources of morality (object, intention, circumstances). Write what changes if each one changes.', 'before-study',
   hook='Ask: “If someone steals to feed their family, is it good, bad or both?” Let students argue before introducing the three sources.',
   teach=['The morality of an act depends on the object (what we choose), the intention (why), and the circumstances (CCC 1750–1754). All three must be good (CCC 1755).', 'A good intention does not make a bad act good; the end does not justify the means (CCC 1753, 1756; Romans 3:8). Some acts are always wrong (intrinsically evil) (CCC 1756).', 'Passions (love, fear, anger, joy) are not bad in themselves; they become good or evil depending on how reason guides them (CCC 1762–1770). Virtue orders the passions (CCC 1767–1770).', 'Circumstances can increase or lessen responsibility but cannot make a bad act good (CCC 1754).'],
   talk=['Why is intention not enough?', 'Which feelings are hardest to guide?', 'Can you think of a case where circumstances change responsibility?'],
   misc=['“If I mean well, it’s fine.”', '“Feelings are sins.”'],
   quiz=['The three sources of morality are…|Object, intention, circumstances|Feelings, plans, luck|Rules, fear, habit|Time, place, people|0|CCC 1750.', 'The end justifies the means is…|Wrong|Right|Sometimes right|Always right|0|CCC 1759.', 'Passions are…|Neither good nor bad in themselves|Always bad|Always good|Irrelevant|0|CCC 1767.'],
   life='Choose one decision this week and examine its object, intention and circumstances.', saint='Thomas Aquinas: he wrote the clearest account of human acts and virtues.', mem='“Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.” (Romans 12:21)', assess='Ask students to analyze a case with the three sources of morality.'),
 L('Law: eternal, natural, Old and New', 'God’s law is the plan of his wisdom. It is shown in the natural law written on our hearts, in the Old Law (Decalogue), and in the New Law of Christ, which is love and the grace of the Spirit.', '1950,1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979', 'Romans 2:14-15;Matthew 5:17-20', 'natural-law moral-law commandments', 'How do natural law, Old Law and New Law relate?', 'Create a chart with columns: eternal law, natural law, Old Law, New Law, civil law. Fill in definitions, sources, purposes and examples. Discuss how civil laws can be just or unjust.', 'peace',
   hook='Ask: “How do you know some things are wrong even without a rule?” Introduce natural law.',
   teach=['The eternal law is God’s plan for the universe (CCC 1950). The natural law is the participation of the rational creature in it, “the light of understanding placed in us by God” (CCC 1954–1960).', 'The Old Law is a preparation for the Gospel; the Decalogue states what reason can know (CCC 1961–1964). The New Law is the grace of the Holy Spirit, the law of love and freedom (CCC 1965–1974).', 'Civil laws bind in conscience when they are just; an unjust law does not oblige (CCC 1902, 2242). Conscientious objection is a duty when laws require evil.', 'Jesus fulfills the law (Matthew 5:17). The Sermon on the Mount is its charter (CCC 1966).'],
   talk=['What is the difference between a rule and a law of love?', 'When might a civil law conflict with natural law?', 'How does grace change the way we keep the law?'],
   misc=['“Natural law is only a Catholic idea.”', '“The New Law abolishes the Ten Commandments.”'],
   quiz=['Natural law is…|Moral law known by reason|A civil law|A custom|A feeling|0|CCC 1954.', 'The New Law is…|The grace of the Holy Spirit and love|A new set of rules|Mere suggestion|Optional|0|CCC 1966.', 'An unjust civil law…|Does not bind in conscience|Always binds|Is fine|Is divine|0|CCC 1903.'],
   life='Read the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5–7) over three days and note one way to live it.', saint='Thomas More: he defended conscience against an unjust law.', mem='“The law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus has set you free.” (Romans 8:2)', assess='Ask students to explain the relationship of the four laws.'),
 L('Grace, justification and merit', 'Grace is God’s free gift that makes us his children. Justification forgives sin and makes us holy. We cooperate with grace, and God rewards what he himself begins in us.', '1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026,2027,2028,2029', 'Ephesians 2:8-10;Philippians 2:12-13', 'grace sanctifying-grace salvation', 'How do grace and effort work together?', 'Study the Catholic and Lutheran agreement in the Joint Declaration on Justification (1999). Write a one-page statement on grace and works, with quotes from Scripture and the Catechism.', 'come-holy-spirit',
   hook='Ask: “Can you earn a gift? What do you do with one?” Use gifts to introduce grace.',
   teach=['Justification is God’s gift, removing sin and communicating righteousness through faith and Baptism (CCC 1987–1995). Its source is God’s mercy and Christ’s sacrifice (CCC 1992).', 'Grace is “the free and undeserved help that God gives us” (CCC 1996). Sanctifying grace makes us holy; actual grace helps us act (CCC 1999–2000). The Spirit works in us “both to will and to work” (Philippians 2:13).', 'Merit: we cannot earn the first grace, but God’s grace enables us to merit further graces and eternal life (CCC 2006–2011). Holiness is the fullness of Christian life (CCC 2012–2016).', 'The 1999 Joint Declaration on Justification shows Catholics and Lutherans share a basic consensus.'],
   talk=['Why is grace a gift and not a wage?', 'What does “work out your salvation with fear and trembling” mean?', 'How should we speak about grace with Protestant friends?'],
   misc=['“Catholics think they earn heaven.”', '“Grace takes away effort.”'],
   quiz=['Grace is…|A free and undeserved gift|A wage|A reward for deeds|A feeling|0|CCC 1996.', 'Can we earn the first grace?|No, it is a gift|Yes, by good works|Sometimes|Never needed|0|CCC 2010.', 'The Joint Declaration on Justification was signed in…|1999|1517|1965|2020|0|CCC context.'],
   life='Thank God for a gift you did not earn and respond with a concrete act of love.', saint='Thérèse of Lisieux: she called her “little way” a path of trust in grace.', mem='“For by grace you have been saved through faith.” (Ephesians 2:8)', assess='Ask students to explain justification, grace and merit in their own words.')]),
 U('The tradition of prayer', 'Prayer', 'How does the Church teach us to pray?', [
 L('The Psalms and Mary at prayer', 'The Psalms are the prayer book of Israel and the Church. Mary’s prayer, from the Annunciation to Pentecost, is a model for our prayer: she listens, says yes and prays with the Church.', '2585,2586,2587,2588,2589,2617,2618,2619,2620,2621,2622,2673,2674,2675,2676,2677,2678,2679,2680,2681,2682', 'Luke 1:46-55;Acts 1:14', 'prayer lectio-divina rosary', 'What do the Psalms and Mary teach about prayer?', 'Pray a Psalm in three ways (aloud, sung and silently). Then pray the Magnificat and write your own “magnificat” of thanks.', 'magnificat',
   hook='Read part of Psalm 22 and Psalm 150. Ask what feelings they show and why the Psalms include both.',
   teach=['The Psalms express every human mood and were Jesus’ prayer book (CCC 2585–2589). The Church prays them in the Mass and the Liturgy of the Hours.', 'Mary’s prayer: the Magnificat (Luke 1:46–55), her presence at Cana, at the Cross and at Pentecost (Acts 1:14) (CCC 2617–2619). She prays for us and with us.', 'The Hail Mary and the Rosary are Marian prayers that lead to Christ (CCC 2676–2678). The Magnificat is evening prayer each day in the Church.', 'Praying the Psalms with Jesus helps us pray in dark times too (Psalm 88).'],
   talk=['Which Psalm speaks to you now?', 'What does the Magnificat reveal about Mary?', 'How does the Rosary help you pray?'],
   misc=['“The Psalms are only for monks.”', '“Mary replaces Christ in prayer.”'],
   quiz=['The Psalms are…|The Church’s prayer book|A history book|A law code|A calendar|0|CCC 2585.', 'The Magnificat is…|Mary’s song of praise|A psalm of David|A creed|A hymn of Paul|0|Luke 1:46.', 'The Rosary leads us to…|Christ|Mary only|Saints only|Silence|0|CCC 2678.'],
   life='Pray one psalm each day this week and the Magnificat in the evening.', saint='Teresa of Calcutta: she began each day with the Magnificat and Mass.', mem='“My soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit rejoices in God my Savior.” (Luke 1:46–47)', assess='Ask students to pray a psalm and explain what it teaches about prayer.'),
 L('Guides in prayer: saints and spiritual traditions', 'The Church has many schools of prayer taught by saints: Benedictine, Franciscan, Ignatian, Carmelite, Salesian and others. They show that there are many ways to pray and the same Spirit teaches all.', '2683,2684,2685,2686,2687,2688,2689,2690,2691,2692,2693,2694,2695,2696', 'Hebrews 12:1-2;Luke 11:1', 'prayer saint discernment', 'Which school of prayer fits me?', 'Sample short practices from three traditions: Benedictine lectio, Ignatian imagination, Carmelite silence. Reflect on which fits, and plan to try one for two weeks.', 'st-ignatius-suscipe',
   hook='Ask students to describe how a favorite athlete or musician practices. Say that saints are coaches in prayer.',
   teach=['The saints are guides in prayer (CCC 2683–2684). Each spiritual family has a charism: Benedictine (liturgy and lectio), Franciscan (simplicity, creation), Dominican (study and preaching), Carmelite (contemplation), Ignatian (discernment and finding God in all things), Salesian (devout life for all).', 'Other guides: the family, pastors, religious communities, catechesis and spiritual direction (CCC 2685–2690). Places and times of prayer support it (CCC 2691–2696).', 'All schools lead to Christ in the Church (CCC 2684). No method replaces relationship.', 'Encourage students to ask a trusted guide before taking on intense practices.'],
   talk=['Who has taught you to pray?', 'What practice would you like to try?', 'Why do we need a guide?'],
   misc=['“There is only one correct way to pray.”', '“Prayer methods are mechanical.”'],
   quiz=['Ignatian spirituality stresses…|Discernment and finding God in all things|Silence only|Chant only|Work only|0|CCC 2684 context.', 'A spiritual director is…|A guide in prayer and life|A judge|A confessor only|A teacher of math|0|CCC 2690.', 'All schools of prayer lead to…|Christ|A method|Fame|Silence|0|CCC 2684.'],
   life='Try one new practice for two weeks and note what you learn, then talk to a mentor.', saint='Francis de Sales: he taught that holiness is for everyone and wrote the Introduction to the Devout Life.', mem='“Lord, teach us to pray.” (Luke 11:1)', assess='Ask students to describe two schools of prayer and choose a practice.'),
 L('Obstacles to prayer and persevering', 'Prayer is a battle against distraction, dryness, discouragement and acedia. Jesus gave parables of persistence, and the Church teaches humility, trust and perseverance. The Our Father is the school of prayer.', '2725,2726,2727,2728,2729,2730,2731,2732,2733,2734,2735,2736,2737,2738,2739,2740,2741,2742,2743,2744,2745,2759,2760,2761', 'Luke 18:1-8;Luke 11:5-13', 'prayer humility forgiveness', 'What helps when prayer is hard?', 'Make a personal “when prayer is hard” card with five practical steps (return, simplify, sit with a psalm, ask a friend to pray, go to the sacraments). Share one with a partner.', 'serenity',
   hook='Read the story of the persistent widow (Luke 18:1–8). Ask: “Why does Jesus tell us this?”',
   teach=['The battle of prayer includes distraction, dryness, presumption and acedia (CCC 2725–2733). A distraction is not a failure; return gently (CCC 2729).', 'Discouragement and doubt about whether God hears can be answered by humility, trust and persistence (CCC 2734–2741). Jesus says, “Ask, and it will be given you” (Luke 11:9).', 'Pray without ceasing means making prayer a habit, supported by liturgy and short prayers (CCC 2742–2745). The Our Father is the model.', 'Seek help if dryness comes with deeper sadness; spiritual direction and counseling both help.'],
   talk=['What is your biggest obstacle in prayer?', 'How do you return after distraction?', 'Why does Jesus tell us to persist?'],
   misc=['“If I’m distracted, I’m not praying.”', '“God doesn’t answer unless I feel something.”'],
   quiz=['A good response to distraction is…|Gently return to God|Quit|Get upset|Skip prayer|0|CCC 2729.', 'Acedia is…|Spiritual laziness or discouragement|A prayer|A feast|A rule|0|CCC 2733.', 'The widow in Luke 18 teaches…|Persistence|Silence|Anger|Fear|0|Luke 18:1.'],
   life='Choose a fixed time and place to pray for two weeks, and when you wander, return gently.', saint='Teresa of Calcutta: she experienced long dryness yet kept praying and serving.', mem='“Pray without ceasing.” (1 Thessalonians 5:17)', assess='Ask students to name three obstacles and a remedy for each.')])],
}
